                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>
  
    <li><p>A herd of 1000 deer decreased to 40% of its previous population every year.</p>
            <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>~t~</m></cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>
  
    <li><p>A herd of 1000 deer decreased by 40% each year. </p>
            <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>~t~</m></cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-4-1-5"><title>Comparing Linear and Exponential Decrease</title>

          <aside component="instructor"><p>After getting students started (point out that only the populations in 1992 and 2000 are relevant to the problem), you might assign the write-up to hand in.</p></aside>

<introduction><p>In 2003, the Center for Biological Diversity filed a lawsuit against the federal government for failing to protect Alaskan sea otters. The population of sea otters, which numbered between 150,000 and 300,000 before hunting began in 1741, declined from about 20,000 in 1992 to 6000 in 2000.</p></introduction>
  
<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>If the population declined linearly after 1992, what was its annual rate of decrease?</p></li>
  
    <li><p> Write a formula for <m>L(t)</m>, the number of otters <m>t</m> years after 1992 under linear decline.</p></li>
  
    <li><p>If the population declined exponentially after 1992, a formula for the number of otters <m>t</m> years after 1992 is <m>~E(t) = 20,000 b^t</m>. Use the population in 2000 to write an equation and solve for <m>b</m>.</p></li>
  
    <li><p>What was the annual decay factor for exponential decline in the otter population? What was its annual percent decrease?</p></li>
  
    <li><p>Predict the number of sea otters in 2010 under each assumption, linear or exponential decline.</p></li>
  
    <li><p>Graph both functions, <m>L(t)</m> and <m>E(t)</m>, on the same grid.</p>  
            <image source="act-7-1-5.png" width="50%"><description>grid [0,20]x[0,25]</description></image></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>In Activity 1, how can you tell from the table of values that the bacteria population is increasing exponentially?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 2, what is the annual growth factor in the cost of a year of college?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 3, what was the decay factor for the town's population each decade? What was the percent decrease in population?</p></li>
    <li><p>If the population of the town in Activity 3 decreased by 500 in the first decade, did it also decrease by 500 in the second decade? Why or why not?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>How can you tell from a table of values that a function is increasing or decreasing exponentially?</p></li>
    <li><p>If an exponential function increases by 500 in the first month, will it also increase by 500 in the second month? Why or why not?</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain why the growth factor for for an exponential function is <m>1+r</m>, where <m>r</m> is given by a percent increase, but the decay factor for a decrease given by percent is <m>1-r</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>If an exponentially decreasing function decreased by 500 this month, will it decrease by more than 500 or less than 500 next month? Why?</p></li>
    <li><p>If you know only two points on a graph, can you determine whether the graph is linear or exponential? Explain.</p></li>
</ol></p>
</assemblage>  

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>A population grows by 4.5% per year. What is its annual growth factor?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>4.5</li>
        <li>0.45</li>
        <li>0.045</li>
        <li>1.045</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>A population decreases by 12% per year. What is its annual decay factor?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>-0.12</li>
        <li>-1.12</li>
        <li>0.88</li>
        <li>1.88</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The function <m>~P(t) = P_0b^t~</m> is decreasing if</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>P_0</m> is negative</li>
        <li><m>b</m> is negative</li>
        <li><m>0 \lt b \lt 1</m></li>
        <li><m>0 \lt P_0 \lt 1</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The graph of <m>~P(t) = P_0b^t</m>, where <m>P_0 \gt 0</m> and <m>b \gt 0</m>, is always</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>increasing.</li>
        <li>concave up.</li>
        <li>decreasing.</li>
        <li>concave down.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>
  
</section>